  "worked_solution": "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $x=g(y),$ the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}[g(y)]^{2}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our equation in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+2 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our example, $g(y)={y}^{2}-2$ and the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=3.$\n\nFinally, the volume can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}{[g(y)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{4}-4{y}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
